Setting (from pp. 91-93). There is a homomorphism of groupoids f: C' -> C. A group μ acts on C' and trivially on C. The groupoid C' carries a central 'internal' action of a group π (written Π on this page). The basic exact sequence is (12) 0 -> π -> Ũ -> U -> 0. Pulling it back along μ -> U gives the commutative extension (13) 0 -> π -> ℰ -> μ -> 0. The context (Teichmüller groupoids with tangential/rigidified base points) strongly suggests that (12) is the exponential sequence 0 -> Z -> R -> U(1) -> 0. In that case μ = μ_n acts by rotation of tangent directions, π = Z is generated by a Dehn twist, and ℰ ≅ (1/n)Z is the group of 'fractional twists'. This reading is my interpretation, not stated on the page.

Result of p. 91. Suppose μ acts freely on the objects of a fibre C'_ξ. Then for objects x, y in the fibre there is a canonical isomorphism of π-torsors Isom_{C'_ξ}(x, y) ≅ ℰ_{y-x}. Here y - x is the unique z ∈ μ with y = z·x, and ℰ_z is the fibre of ℰ over z. Under this isomorphism, composition in C'_ξ corresponds to addition in ℰ.

This page reformulates that result as (15): an isomorphism of π-groupoids C'_ξ ≅ ℰ(ℰ_ξ). Here ℰ_ξ is the μ-torsor of objects over ξ ∈ Ob C = ℰ_0 = ℰ/μ. ℰ(E) denotes the groupoid whose object set is the μ-torsor E, with Hom(x, y) = ℰ_{y-x}. In modern terms this is the action (translation) groupoid E//ℰ, where ℰ acts on E through ℰ -> μ. It is a connected groupoid whose automorphism groups are canonically π, so it is a 'π-connected π-groupoid', i.e. a trivial π-gerbe over a point.

General definition. Fix a central extension (13). A strict (π,μ)-fibration of groupoids subordinate to (13) is a functor f: C' -> C equipped with:
- (1°) an internal π-structure, i.e. a homomorphism π -> Aut(id_{C'}), making π central in every automorphism group;
- (2°) an external μ-action on C' compatible with the π-structure.
These are subject to the following conditions:
- (a) f is a strict π-fibration: the sequences 0 -> π -> Aut_{C'}(x) -> Aut_C(f x) are exact, with surjectivity 'quitte à préciser';
- (b) f is μ-equivariant, with μ acting trivially on C (next page);
- (c) μ acts freely on Ob C' and Ob C'/μ -> Ob C is a bijection;
- (d) a condition on the fibres, developed on p. 93.
On p. 93 the fibres are analysed via the transitive system of isomorphisms H_{x,y} -> H_{αx,αy}. This yields π-torsors H_u (u ∈ μ) with associative isomorphisms H_u ∧ H_v ≅ H_{uv}, i.e. one recovers a central extension of μ by π from the fibre structure.

Purpose. This is a groupoid-level description of how the 'fractional' operations of μ on Teichmüller-type groupoids lift to an extension by the central π (Dehn-twist) action. Grothendieck classifies such lifts by the extension class ℰ in Ext(μ, π).
